Subject: Read the line of a column one time for each line, and not for each token

`ts_lexer__get_column` reads the line again from its start at each call, and `ts_lexer_goto`
invalidates the column data at each move of the lexer. An external scanner that reads the column
one time for each token of a line then takes a time that grows with the square of the length of
the line.

The copy of the runtime of the tree-sitter-cpp fork has this repair.

The C++ scanner of that fork reads the column for each `%`, because `%:` is the digraph of `#`,
and for each name after a declarator. Each measurement below has 50,000 statements on ONE line,
and each file has the same byte count as the file that it compares with:

- `x = a % b;` took 38.889 s, and `x = a + b;` took 0.418 s. The two differ by 93 times.
- `x = a % b;` on 50,000 lines took 0.419 s. The length of the line is the cost.
- `a b;` took 18.989 s on one line, and 0.472 s on 50,000 lines.
- 50,000 functions `void fN() { a<b,c>d; }` of 1.3 MB took 150.7 s.

A profile of a 125 KB file of `a b;` gives `ts_lexer__get_column` 66.4% of the cycles and
`ts_lexer__get_lookahead` 28.2%.

`ColumnAnchor` holds the column of one byte position of one row, from the last computation. The
next computation reads the line from the anchor. A parse that moves forward then reads each line
one time. The anchor is a fact about the text and the included ranges, so `ts_lexer_goto` keeps
it. Only a new input and a new list of included ranges clear it.

The same measurements after the patch: 0.329 s, 0.325 s, 0.322 s, 0.371 s, and 1.279 s. A line of
declarations is then linear in its length: 15 KB 0.019 s, 30 KB 0.037 s, 60 KB 0.075 s, 125 KB
0.160 s, and 250 KB 0.314 s.

On the corpus of 329,387 C++ files and the 7,646 compiler test files, no file gets a different
tree hash, no file gets a parse error, and the silent disagreements with Clang do not change.

Risk for upstream: low. The patch adds 16 bytes to `Lexer`, and it changes no result. A column
that the anchor gives is the column that the walk from the start of the line gives, because the
anchor holds the result of such a walk on the same row.

The patch applies to the upstream tag v0.27.0, after the patches with a smaller number. No other
patch of the series changes lib/src/lexer.c or lib/src/lexer.h. The series compiles with
`-Wall -Wextra` and no warning.

The comments hold the marker "(tree-sitter-cpp fork)". Remove the marker before you send the
patch upstream.

--- a/lib/src/lexer.h	2026-09-16 04:14:45.382098309 +0200
+++ b/lib/src/lexer.h	2026-09-16 04:14:45.383258509 +0200
@@ -15,6 +15,23 @@
   bool valid;
 } ColumnData;
 
+// The character column of one byte position of one row (tree-sitter-cpp fork).
+//
+// `ts_lexer__get_column` reads the line again from its start, and a lexer that moves invalidates
+// `column_data` at each step. A scanner that reads the column one time for each token of a long line
+// then takes a time that grows with the square of the length of the line. The anchor holds the
+// result of the last computation, and the next computation reads the line from the anchor. A parse
+// that moves forward then reads each line one time.
+//
+// The anchor is a fact about the text and the included ranges, and not about the position of the
+// lexer. `ts_lexer_goto` keeps it, and only a new input or a new list of included ranges clears it.
+typedef struct {
+  uint32_t row;
+  uint32_t byte;
+  uint32_t column;
+  bool valid;
+} ColumnAnchor;
+
 typedef struct {
   TSLexer data;
   Length current_position;
@@ -33,6 +50,7 @@
   uint32_t lookahead_size;
   bool did_get_column;
   ColumnData column_data;
+  ColumnAnchor column_anchor;
 
   char debug_buffer[TREE_SITTER_SERIALIZATION_BUFFER_SIZE];
 } Lexer;
